    <item>
      <title>Recapping and resetting my strategy</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/recap-and-reset/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 27 Feb 2025 12:37:58 -0800</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/recap-and-reset/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m 3 weeks in to my coding journey. It&amp;rsquo;s been a blast so far. Robert Frost had&#xA;some line about &amp;ldquo;Making my advocation my vocation&amp;rdquo;. It&amp;rsquo;s great when you find&#xA;something in life that you&amp;rsquo;re so excited about you want to give your all to it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;After 3 weeks I had solved a whopping 45 leetcode problems. I could tell I was&#xA;into it. Being a noobie, obviously I was using a lot of resources, especially&#xA;AI, to help.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Blogging my journey</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/blogging-my-journey/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 14 Feb 2025 12:37:54 -0800</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/blogging-my-journey/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;About a week into my code learning journey I got a tip from a friend. He shared&#xA;his repo where he&amp;rsquo;d stored all his leetcode solutions. This was brilliant. The&#xA;site itself is pretty easy to use, but taking the time to copy out my solutions,&#xA;then work through commenting them, and saving them somewhere I could share? A no&#xA;brainer.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;After setting up my own repo of solutions it dawned on me: I might like to write&#xA;a bit about my journey. I&amp;rsquo;m sure no one will read it. All the better. The&#xA;internet needs more personal websites.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Learning to code</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/kick-off/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 07 Feb 2025 16:35:44 -0800</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/kick-off/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;On the last day of January this year I set out to learn to code. This is not the&#xA;first time I&amp;rsquo;ve been down this path. But&amp;hellip; the stars aligned and I got a very&#xA;helpful email from a friend who set me on my way.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The email? It mentioned 2 important things:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;nothing beats hard work&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;try out leetcode&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Hard work. Really? Wait. I always thought of coding and software as producing&#xA;&amp;ldquo;smart solutions&amp;rdquo;. Whereas, I chalked up myself and my approaches as hardway,&#xA;slow, inferior. I don&amp;rsquo;t know why (lack of brain cells), but I never sat down and&#xA;gave learning to code an honest days hard work. It&amp;rsquo;s funny how a quick mention&#xA;can have a huge impact.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
